ce thing  when everyone swaps bodies and yeah okay  that happens they swap bodies that's how  polymorph ends up in the tortoise i  really liked that you could tell which  personality was in which body  just by looking at the posing of the  respective characters that was a really  cool visual touch  and okay it's time to discuss the ending  now the ending  those among you that have taken the time  to watch any of my other reviews will  notice that when something impresses me  i  tend to be more critical of it that  shouldn't be taken as any indication  other than respect  when a story surprises me i like to up  my default standard to test where the  merits of the story truly lie  for instance i'm not going to heavily  scrutinize over the idiosyncrasies of  phantom blood's narrative because  it's a simple story exercising quite  well and established formula while  adding in some interesting things here  and there  and in the case of stardust crusaders  it's clear what araki was going for and  when the ending came about it fit that  building quite well  in other words trying to get a read on  what the ark is trying to do is key in  determining for me if it's successful or  not in its execution but when it comes  to stories like  golden wind i think there's enough going  on under the surface to warrant holding  the expertise of a rocky to a higher  standard and keeping that in mind  because  of this i have to say that i thought the  ending to golden wind in addition to  being disappointing  on a narrative level personally also  felt confused  convenient and felt as though it  struggled to tie anything together  and as a result made other earlier  choices in the story feel either  tagged in or totally undelivered upon  but i want to make one thing clear  this isn't the same thing as a show  delivering something good with it not  being what i particularly liked  or being let down by comparison i mean i  never would have expected part four to  go the way that it went  and 